#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203684 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/id #uuid "53571345-258b-54e1-af9c-fa68d0d0f40e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14613" :question/problem "Let $A = \\{4, 6, 12 \\}$. Construct a graph for the following relation:\n\n$R = \\{(x,y) \\in A^2 \\::\\: x \\mid y\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where\n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ with $x \\mid y$.\n\n- If $x = 4$, then the values $y = 4, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,4)$ and $(4,12)$, and we should plot these points on the graph.\n- If $x = 6$, then the values $y = 6, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(6,6)$ and $(6,12)$, and we should plot these points on the graph.\n- If $x = 12$, then the value $y = 12$ satisfies $x \\mid y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(12,12)$, and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/48/48836a0157b46f3f7af3ceb0db094612794b7da6c2d749bd8769f87da410b0d6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203685 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/id #uuid "5c1451cc-7b5c-5f8f-9dfb-60c18319bce8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14614" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d07b5e999e4f0c9f403bf5ac3d61dfd12e1accd11a72d68a2f3b39196a58060.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{1, 3, 5 \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n1. $(1, 5)$\n2. $(5, 5)$\n3. $(3, 1)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where\n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(1, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $1$ and ends at the node $5$.\n- The pair $(5, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $5$ to itself.\n- The pair $(3, 1)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $3$ and ends at the node $1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/id #uuid "9306fe02-b1dd-584c-852f-27778aed9d8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14650" :question/problem "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B$, given by\n\n$A=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}$.\n\nConstruct a mapping diagram for the following relation:\n$R= \\big\\{(x,y) \\in A \\times B \\:: \\: x\\cdot y \\geq 4 \\big\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A \\times B$ with\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y \\geq 4\n$$\n\n- If $x=1$, then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x\\cdot y \\geq 4$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,5)$, and we should add the arrow $1 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n- If $x=2$, then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2)$ and $(2,5)$, and we should add the arrows $2 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n- If $x=3$, then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,5)$, and we should add the arrows $3 \\rightarrow 2$ and $3 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/7d/7ddf9a6fb23a475e75128b3c4bce145b5488eb5c12dfe592578cdf10435dc1a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1e04f26-6940-5987-a064-fca37286e3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200026} {:answer/id #uuid "36dd7548-8046-5bc9-b37e-c8c20645fa54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200027} {:answer/id #uuid "61932268-a45f-51b3-b5b8-0a90c92f78e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200028} {:answer/id #uuid "712530cf-9493-50ee-9ab9-b3cce719f722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200029} {:answer/id #uuid "74d6da5e-a3b4-551f-91c1-90d21542865a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200030}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6e20a531-2863-5d75